tput_tail": "`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\n**Why it was wrong:** the float `2.675` is actually stored as `2.67499999999999982\u2026`, so `round(x, 2)` rounded it down. `round()` also rounds exact halves to the nearest even digit, which isn't the \"half up\" behaviour the docstring promises.\n\n**Fix** (`pricing/money.py`): the function now turns `x` into a `Decimal` using `repr(x)`. That gives the shortest string that maps back to the same float (`\"2.675\"`). It then rounds to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP` and returns a `float`, so callers get the same type as before.\n\n**Checked:**\n- 2.675 \u2192 2.68, 2.665 \u2192 2.67, 0.125 \u2192 0.13, 1.005 \u2192 1.01, 2.674 \u2192 2.67.\n- -2.675 \u2192 -2.68: halves round away from zero, so negative amounts round to the larger magnitude.\n- `add_vat` gives the same results as before on 10.0 and 2.5.\n- pytest isn't installed, so I ran the existing `test_split` directly and it passes.\n\n**Still open:** if the input to `round_money` is already off because of earlier float maths (like `x * (1 + rate)` in `add_vat`), `repr` keeps that error. A fully exact fix would mean doing the price arithmetic in `Decimal` throughout, which I didn't do here.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 3, "replies_ok": 7, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 159317, "requests_cache_write": 40066, "requests_cost_usd": 0.26857739999999997, "requests_count": 7, "requests_input_uncached": 16, "requests_output": 1816, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial2", "run_seed": 20261014, "sampling_observed": null, "schedule_index": 2, "score": 1.0, "started_at": "2026-10-13T04:22:22.770204+00:00", "subagent_resumed": false, "subagent_write_calls": 0, "success": true, "t_agent_s": 24.253, "t_checker_s": 0.395, "t_env_setup_s": 0.001, "task": "trig-omp-bugfix-regress", "task_calls": 0, "thinking_mode": "default", "thinking_se